Distributed Transactions

Distributed transactions are transactions that span multiple nodes in a distributed system. They are a fundamental concept in distributed systems, as they allow for the coordination of operations across multiple nodes, ensuring that either all operations are completed successfully, or none are. This is crucial for maintaining consistency in a distributed system.

In this chapter, we will explore the concepts, algorithms, and implementation details of distributed transactions. We will cover the two-phase commit (2PC) and three-phase commit (3PC) protocols, which are the most commonly used protocols for implementing distributed transactions.

Distributed Transactions

A distributed transaction is a transaction that involves operations on multiple nodes in a distributed system. The transaction must ensure that all operations are completed successfully, or none are. This is known as the all-or-nothing property of transactions.

Consider a simple example of a bank transfer between two accounts. The transfer involves two operations: debiting the source account and crediting the destination account. If these accounts are on different nodes, the transaction is a distributed transaction. The transaction must ensure that either both operations are completed, or neither is. If the debit operation succeeds but the credit operation fails, money would be lost. If the credit operation succeeds but the debit operation fails, money would be created.

The challenge in implementing distributed transactions is that the nodes in a distributed system can fail independently. A node might fail after it has committed its part of the transaction but before other nodes have committed theirs. This can lead to inconsistencies.

To handle these challenges, distributed systems use commit protocols, which are algorithms that coordinate the commit or abort of a distributed transaction across multiple nodes. The most commonly used commit protocols are the two-phase commit (2PC) and three-phase commit (3PC) protocols.

Two-Phase Commit

The two-phase commit (2PC) protocol is a commit protocol that ensures the all-or-nothing property of distributed transactions. It is called "two-phase" because it involves two phases: a voting phase and a commit phase.

In the voting phase, a coordinator node asks all participant nodes whether they are prepared to commit the transaction. Each participant checks whether it can commit the transaction, and if so, it writes a "prepared" record to its log and responds "yes" to the coordinator. If a participant cannot commit the transaction, it responds "no" and the transaction is aborted.

In the commit phase, if all participants responded "yes" in the voting phase, the coordinator writes a "commit" record to its log and sends a "commit" message to all participants. Each participant writes a "commit" record to its log and commits the transaction. If any participant responded "no" in the voting phase, the coordinator writes an "abort" record to its log and sends an "abort" message to all participants. Each participant writes an "abort" record to its log and aborts the transaction.

The 2PC protocol ensures the all-or-nothing property of transactions, but it has a major drawback: it is a blocking protocol. If the coordinator fails after the voting phase but before the commit phase, the participants are left in an uncertain state. They have written "prepared" records to their logs, but they do not know whether to commit or abort the transaction. They must wait for the coordinator to recover, which can take an arbitrarily long time. During this time, the participants hold locks on the resources involved in the transaction, which can lead to a deadlock.

Three-Phase Commit

The three-phase commit (3PC) protocol is a commit protocol that addresses the blocking problem of 2PC. It is called "three-phase" because it involves three phases: a voting phase, a pre-commit phase, and a commit phase.

In the voting phase, the coordinator asks all participants whether they are prepared to commit the transaction, just as in 2PC. Each participant responds "yes" or "no".

In the pre-commit phase, if all participants responded "yes" in the voting phase, the coordinator sends a "pre-commit" message to all participants. Each participant writes a "pre-commit" record to its log and responds "ack" to the coordinator. The pre-commit phase is a buffer between the voting phase and the commit phase. It ensures that if the coordinator fails, the participants can elect a new coordinator and complete the transaction.

In the commit phase, when the coordinator has received "ack" from all participants, it sends a "commit" message to all participants. Each participant writes a "commit" record to its log and commits the transaction.

The 3PC protocol is non-blocking, meaning that participants do not have to wait for the coordinator to recover if it fails. However, 3PC has its own drawbacks. It requires more communication than 2PC, and it is more complex. It also assumes a synchronous system with bounded message delays, which is not always realistic in practice. In a real system, network partitions can occur, and 3PC can lead to inconsistencies in the presence of partitions. For these reasons, 3PC is not commonly used in practice. Instead, systems use variations of 2PC that handle failures in a more practical way, such as Paxos Commit or Raft-based commit protocols.

In the following sections, we will explore the 2PC and 3PC protocols in detail, including their algorithms, their failure handling, and their implementation.

Two-Phase Commit

The two-phase commit (2PC) protocol is a commit protocol that ensures the all-or-nothing property of distributed transactions. It is the most commonly used commit protocol in practice, due to its simplicity and its ability to handle a wide range of failures.

In this section, we will explore the 2PC protocol in detail. We will start with the basic protocol, then we will discuss how it handles failures, and finally we will provide an implementation.

The Basic Protocol

The 2PC protocol involves two types of nodes: a coordinator and participants. The coordinator is the node that initiates the transaction, and the participants are the nodes that are involved in the transaction. In a typical scenario, the coordinator is the node where the transaction is submitted, and the participants are the nodes where the transaction's operations are executed.

The protocol proceeds in two phases:

Voting Phase: The coordinator sends a "prepare" message to all participants. Each participant processes the transaction's operations, but does not commit them. If a participant can commit the transaction, it writes a "prepared" record to its log, and responds "yes" to the coordinator. If a participant cannot commit the transaction (e.g., because of a conflict with another transaction), it responds "no" to the coordinator. The participant is now "prepared" to commit or abort the transaction, depending on the coordinator's decision.

Commit Phase: If the coordinator receives "yes" from all participants, it writes a "commit" record to its log, and sends a "commit" message to all participants. Each participant writes a "commit" record to its log, and commits the transaction. If the coordinator receives "no" from any participant (or if a participant fails to respond within a timeout), it writes an "abort" record to its log, and sends an "abort" message to all participants. Each participant writes an "abort" record to its log, and aborts the transaction.

The key insight of 2PC is the "prepared" state. When a participant writes a "prepared" record to its log, it is making a promise: it promises that it can commit the transaction if the coordinator decides to commit, and it promises that it can abort the transaction if the coordinator decides to abort. This promise is what allows the coordinator to make a global decision (commit or abort) that all participants will follow.

Failure Handling

The 2PC protocol is designed to handle failures of the coordinator and the participants. The protocol's behavior in the presence of failures depends on when the failure occurs.

Coordinator Failure: If the coordinator fails before the voting phase, the participants will not receive a "prepare" message, and they will abort the transaction after a timeout. If the coordinator fails after the voting phase but before the commit phase, the participants are in the "prepared" state, and they do not know whether to commit or abort. They must wait for the coordinator to recover. If the coordinator fails after the commit phase, the participants will receive the coordinator's decision from the coordinator's log when it recovers.

Participant Failure: If a participant fails before the voting phase, the coordinator will not receive a "yes" response, and it will abort the transaction. If a participant fails after the voting phase but before the commit phase, the participant is in the "prepared" state. When it recovers, it must ask the coordinator for the decision. If the participant fails after the commit phase, it will recover based on its log.

Network Partition: If a network partition occurs, the coordinator and the participants may not be able to communicate. The protocol's behavior depends on the partition. If the coordinator is partitioned from some participants, the coordinator will abort the transaction (because it did not receive "yes" from all participants). If a participant is partitioned from the coordinator, the participant will be in the "prepared" state, and it will have to wait for the coordinator to recover.

The most problematic failure scenario is when the coordinator fails after the voting phase but before the commit phase. In this scenario, the participants are in the "prepared" state, and they must wait for the coordinator to recover. This is the blocking problem of 2PC. During this time, the participants hold locks on the resources involved in the transaction, which can lead to a deadlock.

The blocking problem of 2PC is a fundamental trade-off. To ensure the all-or-nothing property of transactions, the participants must not make a unilateral decision (commit or abort) without knowing the coordinator's decision. This means that they must wait for the coordinator if it fails. The three-phase commit protocol, which we will discuss in the next section, addresses this problem by adding an additional phase that allows the participants to elect a new coordinator if the original coordinator fails.

Implementation

Let's implement the 2PC protocol in Rust. We'll implement both the coordinator and the participant, and we'll use a simple message-passing model for communication.

First, let's define the messages that are exchanged between the coordinator and the participants.

