3PC Timeout Mechanism

The key insight of 3PC is the timeout mechanism. If a participant times out waiting for a message, it can make a decision based on its current state:

If the participant is in the Init state, it aborts (because it hasn't voted yet).

If the participant is in the Prepared state, it aborts (because it hasn't received a pre-commit yet).

If the participant is in the Pre-Commit state, it commits (because it has received a pre-commit, which means all participants voted Yes).

This ensures that all participants make the same decision, even if the coordinator fails.

3PC Limitations

While 3PC addresses the blocking problem of 2PC, it has its own limitations:

Network Partitions: 3PC does not handle network partitions well. If the network partitions, different partitions may make different decisions, leading to inconsistency.

Higher Latency: 3PC requires three rounds of communication, which increases latency compared to 2PC.

More Complex: 3PC is more complex to implement than 2PC.

Because of these limitations, 3PC is not widely used in practice. Instead, most systems use 2PC with additional mechanisms to handle failures, such as Paxos Commit or Raft-based consensus.

